$DIR/main.p:10:2: cannot pass &x to f (which may call (k).Keep), which keeps it after returning
    &x is a pointer to x. x is a local variable of main (declared at main.p:9).
    f (which may call (k).Keep) stores p in package variable saved (main.p:6),
    so &x would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If f (which may call (k).Keep) should keep its own copy of x, change it to
    take the value, not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[int] from
    new, a shared[int], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
